	<td bgcolor="#D6CDA3" width="100%" valign="top">
	<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1"> <b>What does all of this mean to you?</b></font>
	<p><font face="verdana, arial, helvetica" size="2">If these equations are correct (and HQ collects some of the best reverse engineers I have ever seen) Some observations spring to mind:<br />
<br />
Let's look at mitigation first:<br />
(factors ranked by overall impact on AC.)<br />
(VAC == visible AC)<br />
1. Equipment AC (1 or 1.57 VAC per equipment AC)<br />
2. Defense (I assume this means the skill) (.5 or .33 VAC per skill point)<br />
3. Buff AC (.33 or .25 VAC per buff AC)<br />
<br />
Now avoidance:<br />
(factors ranked by overall impact on AC)<br />
1. Defense (2.10 VAC per skill point)<br />
2. Agility (fixed: -28.33 - 73.2 depending on level and base agility score)<br />
<br />
Which would lead one to believe that overwhelmingly Defense plays the largest role in one's visible AC (between 2.43 and 2.60 VAC per point). Moral of this overly lengthy story? Keep your defense up.<br />
<br />
So what's most important to visible AC? Based on these equations I would rank them as follows:<br />
<br />
1. Defense<br />
2. Equipment AC<br />
NOTE: in the case of very high AC equipment equipment AC will be more important than defense.<br />
3. Buff AC<br />
4. Agility (caveat: agility will play a large role at the lowest levels but will quickly drop down to little significance)<br />
<br />
Anyone want to double-check my math?<br />
<br />
Now. How does AC actually figure into combat? Anyone?<br />
<br />
Thanks for an informative post, Graal <img src="images/smilies/smile.gif" border="0" alt=""><br />
<br />
-Laod<br />
<br />
Edit:<br />
I realized somewhat belatedly that it's possible to get a lot more points of equipment AC than defense in the long run. So, if you have uber equipment it may well be making more of a difference than your defense. Sorry for that error <img src="images/smilies/smile.gif" border="0" alt=""></font></p>
	
